# pnpm global config. Symlinked to ~/.config/pnpm/rc by install.sh (lib/manifest.sh).
#
# Adopted into the repo 2026-08-07. Before that this was a plain untracked file from
# 2026-03-20 sitting in a config layer nothing installed, linked, diffed or backed up —
# so the malformed allowlist below went unnoticed for months. That is the exact defect
# class lib/manifest.sh was written for; it just never covered pnpm.

# pnpm expands ${HOME} in rc values.
global-bin-dir=${HOME}/.local/bin

# --- build-script allowlist --------------------------------------------------
#
# pnpm 10 blocks dependency install/preinstall/postinstall scripts by default. Only the
# packages named here may run one.
#
# THE SYNTAX IS LOAD-BEARING AND FAILS SILENTLY. This file previously held:
#
#     only-built-dependencies="['@tailwindcss/oxide','@sentry/cli','supabase','puppeteer']"
#
# pnpm parsed that as ONE OPAQUE STRING, not a four-element array, so the allowlist
# approved ZERO packages and every build script on the box stayed blocked. There is no
# warning, no parse error, no log line — a wrong value here is indistinguishable from a
# correct one until something fails to build.
#
# Only two forms parse as a real array (verified empirically against pnpm 10.33.2, and
# against its bundled source: types.js declares this key as `[String]`):
#   1. repeated `only-built-dependencies[]=pkg` lines   <- used here, one-line diffs
#   2. single-quoted bare JSON: only-built-dependencies='["a","b"]'
# Comma-separated and unquoted-JSON forms both silently degrade back to a string.
#
# VERIFY AFTER ANY EDIT — do not trust this file by eye:
#     pnpm config get only-built-dependencies --json
# It must print a real JSON array. The non-json form printing pnpm's "would display an
# array as comma-separated list" WARN is the positive signal that it parsed as an array.
#
# This rc is also the ONLY place that works for global installs: for any `--global`
# command pnpm deletes workspaceDir and forcibly overwrites manifest-derived build
# settings with the rc-derived value, so onlyBuiltDependencies in the global package.json
# or a pnpm-workspace.yaml beside it is inert. Don't "fix" it there.
only-built-dependencies[]=@tailwindcss/oxide
only-built-dependencies[]=@sentry/cli
only-built-dependencies[]=supabase
only-built-dependencies[]=puppeteer
# Added 2026-08-07. @ast-grep/cli ships a 261-byte shim as its `ast-grep` entrypoint and
# relies on postinstall.js hardlinking the real ~48MB binary from its platform package
# over the top. With builds blocked the shim survived, and `ast-grep` exited 1 with a
# decoy message in 43 distinct agent sessions across 27 checkouts before anyone reported
# it (nobody did — grep is a perfect silent fallback). Its postinstall is a pure local
# hardlink-or-copy: no network, no compiler, deterministic. Safe to allow.
only-built-dependencies[]=@ast-grep/cli
#
# THE RULE FOR ADDING TO THIS LIST: allow a package whose install script is deterministic and
# local (unpack, hardlink, copy). Do NOT allow one that fetches a prebuilt native module or
# falls back to a node-gyp compile — that turns a silent degradation into a possible hard
# install failure on a box without the toolchain.
#
# The worked example was keytar and node-pty. Both were broken here, both were left out on
# exactly that reasoning, and both left with `pnpm rm -g @google/gemini-cli` on 2026-08-07 —
# they were its exclusive optionalDependencies and nothing else on the box wanted them.
# Neither is installed now, so neither needs an entry; the rule is what survives.

# --- supply-chain release cooldown -------------------------------------------
#
# Refuse packages published less than 1440 minutes (24h) ago, so a compromised release
# has to survive a detection window before it can install here. 1440 is what a private app repository's
# pnpm-workspace.yaml already uses, and is the default pnpm 11 ships on by default.
#
# SAME SYNTAX TRAP AS THE ALLOWLIST ABOVE, different shape. Measured on pnpm 10.33.2:
#     minimum-release-age=1440   ->  pnpm config get minimumReleaseAge  ->  1440
#     minimumReleaseAge=1440     ->  pnpm config get minimumReleaseAge  ->  undefined
# The camelCase spelling is accepted, stored, and completely inert. No warning.
#
# VERIFY AFTER ANY EDIT — do not trust this file by eye:
#     pnpm config get minimumReleaseAge      # must print 1440, never undefined
minimum-release-age=1440
#
# WHAT THIS ACTUALLY DOES — all four measured against the live registry on 10.33.2,
# because the two failure modes look nothing alike:
#   * An EXACT pin that is too young hard-fails: ERR_PNPM_NO_MATURE_MATCHING_VERSION.
#   * A RANGE or dist-tag that is too young silently resolves one release back, exit 0,
#     with only pnpm's generic "(1.2.3 is available)" note — which is byte-identical to
#     an ordinary outdated-dependency hint and says nothing about the cooldown.
#   * `pnpm dlx tool@latest` also silently serves the older release, and prints NOTHING
#     at all. That is the quietest path here: a script expecting today's release gets
#     yesterday's with no signal. Nothing on this box currently does that (the one
#     `pnpm dlx` in hooks/lib/package-manager.js is a descriptor-table string,
#     not a live invocation), but check that before adding one.
#   * `pnpm install --frozen-lockfile` skips resolution entirely, so the cooldown is
#     NOT evaluated and CI is unaffected. The corollary matters: already-locked deps get
#     no retroactive protection. This guards new adds and dependency bumps, nothing else.
#
# SCOPE: a global value here applies to `pnpm install` in any project with no local
# override, to `pnpm add -g`, and to `pnpm dlx` — all three verified by real installs,
# not by reading config. A project's own pnpm-workspace.yaml fully OVERRIDES this value
# in both directions (a project setting 0 disables it; a project setting more is stricter),
# so a private app repository keeps deciding for itself and its matching 1440 changes nothing there.
#
# ESCAPE HATCH for a genuine urgent install inside the window — verified working:
#     pnpm add -g --config.minimum-release-age=0 <pkg>
# Prefer that over editing this file. pnpm gives no hint that this flag exists or that a
# version was ever filtered, so a future operator will not find it without this comment.
#
# If some package ever needs a standing exemption, the exclude list takes the SAME
# bracket-suffix form as the allowlist above, and degrades the same silent way:
#     minimum-release-age-exclude[]=some-pkg     # parses as a real array
#     minimum-release-age-exclude=some-pkg       # parses as a bare string, protects nothing
# Deliberately empty right now: every standing exemption is a permanent hole, and the
# per-invocation flag above covers the real urgent case without leaving one.
#
# THE ONE AUTOMATED PER-RUN EXCEPTION: scripts/codex-update.sh (dotfiles#1377)
# adopts stable Codex releases the day they ship. It never edits this file and never
# sets the age to 0: each install gets a private npm_config_userconfig rc naming EXACT
# @openai/codex versions — the candidate plus every native alias its manifest declares,
# because pnpm checks all of them, not just the host's. Measured 2026-09-26 on 10.33.2:
# the --config.minimum-release-age-exclude CLI forms are silently ignored and a cwd
# .npmrc does not reach `add -g`; under the userconfig override this file still applies.
